M61 = [("", False), (
"Major elements of Australasian tektites were measured by electron probe microanalysis (EPMA; 153 spot analyses on seven "
"tektites: MNG24_A3_1–3, MNG24_B1, MNG24_C2 and MG24_B1 from three localities near Muong Nong, Savannakhet Province, Laos "
"(~16.5°N, 106.5°E), and KK24_T1 from Khon Kaen, northeastern Thailand) [instrument, accelerating voltage, beam current, beam "
"diameter and standards to be supplied by P. Sanchez]. "
"Because tektite glass is essentially anhydrous, analyses with oxide totals below 97 wt% were rejected as compromised by "
"vesicles, alteration or polishing relief, following the screening criterion of Carter et al. (2025). This retains 82 "
"analyses on four tektites (MNG24_A3_1, n = 30; MNG24_B1, n = 17; MG24_B1, n = 5; KK24_T1, n = 30). Every analysis of "
"MNG24_A3_2 (totals 33–86 wt%), MNG24_A3_3 (61–97 wt%) and MNG24_C2 (66–94 wt%) was rejected, as were four marginal analyses "
"(96.3–96.9 wt%) of MNG24_B1 and MG24_B1 (Fig. 14a). "
"Accepted analyses were recomputed anhydrous with total Fe as FeO. Trace elements were measured by laser-ablation ICP-MS on "
"two tektites from sample MNG24_A3 (MNG24_A3_1 and MNG24_A3_2; five ablation sites each, five replicate analyses per site) "
"using EPMA SiO2 as the internal standard [instrument, calibration and secondary reference materials to be supplied]. Three "
"MNG24_A3_2 sites had been standardised to SiO2 values (27–37 wt%) taken from rejected EPMA points; because Al/Si, which is "
"independent of the internal standard, is identical in the two tektites, all MNG24_A3_2 analyses were re-standardised to the "
"accepted MNG24_A3_1 mean (75.0 wt% SiO2). Relative to EPMA on the same tektite, LA-ICP-MS returns Al2O3, MgO and TiO2 "
"30, 7 and 8 % high and MnO 38 % low, and most trace-element concentrations lie 3–38 % above the median of published "
"Indochinite and Muong Nong analyses (Cr is 18 % below it; Ni is twice it, though within the published range) (Fig. 16a). Absolute LA-ICP-MS concentrations are therefore treated as accurate to no "
"better than ±30 %, and interpretation relies on inter-element ratios (Th/Sc, Eu/Eu*, rare-earth pattern shape) that are "
"insensitive to the internal standard and to most calibration offsets. Candidate target lithologies sampled in this study – "
"Khorat Group sandstone (n = 10) and mudstone (n = 3), lateritic saprolite developed on basalt (n = 4), the tektite-hosting "
"diamicton or “catastro-loess” (n = 9) and Bolaven basalt (n = 2) – were analysed for major elements by X-ray fluorescence "
"and for trace elements by ICP-MS on pressed-powder pellets. They were combined with the regional compilation of Sieh et al. "
"(2019), which includes analyses from Sanematsu et al. (2011) and Saminpanya et al. (2014) among other sources: Bolaven basalt (n = 252 "
"after removing 62 analyses with LOI > 3 wt% or totals outside 98.5–101.5 wt%), "
"Khorat Group sandstone (49) and mudstone (5), laterite and saprolite on basalt (47), sedimentary laterite and bauxite (37), "
"Bolaven loess (13; grouped here with the diamicton) and felsic metatuff (8; dacitic–rhyolitic, Na2O 2.6–5.7 and K2O 3.0–5.5 wt%, "
"major elements only). Three further samples labelled metatuff in the compilation were excluded: two are altered mafic rocks "
"(SiO2 43–57 wt%, LOI 8–9 wt%) and one is Na-leached (Na2O 0.01 wt%). The field context of the metatuff (unit, and whether it "
"lies beneath the Khorat Group) is not documented in the compilation. Values reported below detection were set to half the "
"detection limit. The basalts were divided at the alkaline–subalkaline "
"boundary of Macdonald & Katsura (1964) into tholeiitic basalt and basaltic andesite (n = 229) and alkali basalt and basanite "
"(n = 25, including both basalts analysed here), because the two suites differ several-fold in incompatible-element "
"contents (median Th 1.6 versus 7.0 µg/g; Th/Sc 0.07 versus 0.46). Published Indochinite and Muong Nong-type tektite analyses (n = 262; e.g. Koeberl, 1992; "
"Amare & Koeberl, 2006) provide the regional reference.")]

M62 = [("", False), (
"Tektite source lithologies were inferred with the reversible-jump Bayesian unmixing model of Carter et al. (2025), "
"re-implemented here (code archived with the data). Each EPMA analysis is modelled as a mixture, Y = C·M, of K components "
"whose compositions (C) and per-analysis mixing weights (M) are unknown; K itself is a free parameter (Gallagher et al., 2009). "
"Following Carter et al. (2025), each component is drawn from a dictionary of lithology types, each type is assigned a "
"truncated-normal prior on every oxide, the prior on K is Poisson with mean 10 (truncated at 30), the mixing weights of each "
"analysis carry a Dirichlet prior, and the likelihood is Gaussian on centred-log-ratio-transformed data and model. The "
"dictionary comprises the eight regional lithology types listed above (prior medians and robust standard deviations (1.4826 × MAD) from our analyses and the regional compilation of Sieh et al. (2019), with "
"standard deviations of at least 15 % of the median, or 25 % for types with fewer than five analyses), an unweathered "
"post-Archean shale (PAAS; Taylor & McLennan, 1985; ±20 %) to represent fine-grained sediment not affected by tropical "
"weathering, and an unconstrained “random” component. Nine oxides were used (SiO2, TiO2, Al2O3, FeOt, MnO, MgO, CaO, "
"Na2O, K2O); Cl, SO3 and Cr2O3 are at or below detection. Where Carter et al. (2025) used a uniform 0.5 % relative "
"uncertainty, we combined per-oxide EPMA accuracy (1–5 %, 20 % for MnO) in quadrature with a 5 % mixing-model error, "
"because tektite melting does not conserve every oxide exactly. Posterior sampling used Metropolis–Hastings with "
"within-model moves (composition, weights, component-type swap) and between-model moves (birth, death, split and merge), "
"step sizes adapted to an acceptance rate of 0.234 during burn-in. As in Carter et al. (2025), twelve chains were "
"started from random states with K drawn uniformly from 2–30 and run for "
"6 × 10⁴ iterations, the first half being discarded as burn-in. Once a chain has converged, between-model moves are "
"almost never accepted, because adding or removing a component perturbs the fit to all 82 analyses at once; the "
"posterior on K and on component identity is therefore represented by the spread among chains. Chains whose median "
"log-likelihood lay more than 50 units below that of the best chain (i.e. trapped in a much poorer mode) would have been "
"excluded; none were. Uncertainties combine within- and between-"
"chain variance (Carter et al., 2025). Sensitivity "
"runs (four chains each, 3 × 10⁴ iterations) excluded the random component, excluded Na2O and K2O, and restricted the "
"dictionary to the regional lithologies (no PAAS or random component). Trace elements were not "
"inverted. Instead, the bulk posterior mixtures were combined with the median trace-element contents of each lithology to "
"predict Th/Sc and Eu/Eu*, which were then compared with the LA-ICP-MS data.")]
